-- PURPOSE-SCHEMA RELOCATION — UPGRADE PATH ONLY (GH #862).
--
-- This file is listed in manifest.upgrade and NOT in manifest.install, for the
-- same reason indexes/drop_entities_tier_btree.sql.in is: a fresh database has
-- nothing to relocate. Every function below is created directly at its purpose
-- address by the install script, so on CREATE EXTENSION this work does not
-- exist. It exists only to carry an ALREADY-INSTALLED substrate forward.
--
-- WHY ALTER ... SET SCHEMA AND NOT DROP + CREATE.
--
-- PostgreSQL records dependencies by OID. A view, an index expression, an ACL,
-- a COMMENT, and another function's stored parse tree all reference a function
-- through pg_proc.oid, never through its name. ALTER FUNCTION ... SET SCHEMA is
-- a catalog update: the row keeps its OID, so every one of those dependents
-- follows the move silently and nothing has to be rebuilt.
--
-- DROP FUNCTION laplace.f + CREATE FUNCTION purpose.f mints a NEW oid. Every
-- dependent then either blocks the drop (RESTRICT, the default -- this is the
-- 2BP01 that entities_highway_bits_gin raised against
-- laplace.laplace_highway_mask_bits) or is destroyed by CASCADE. Compatibility
-- wrappers patch that symptom while permanently doubling the API surface, which
-- is the exact condition #862 exists to remove. So: relocate, never re-create.
--
-- IDEMPOTENT BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY DEMOLITION. The loop reads what is
-- actually in pg_proc. On a second run the source schema no longer holds the
-- function, the cursor yields nothing, and the block is a no-op. Overloads are
-- handled because pg_get_function_identity_arguments enumerates each signature
-- separately. Nothing is dropped, so re-running can never lose a dependent.
--
-- ORDERING: this must run BEFORE the function definition files in
-- manifest.upgrade. It moves the old body to the new address; the definition
-- files that follow then CREATE OR REPLACE that same address, which updates the
-- body in place and again preserves the OID.
DO $relocate$
DECLARE
    m record;
    r record;
